1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,728
Aziz has been our hero for thirty-seven lessons, but he is not a real person.

2
00:00:04,778 --> 00:00:11,016
The test team needs him every day: registered, proved, blocked, refreshed, and proved again.

3
00:00:11,066 --> 00:00:16,200
MyID, though, is a state service that proves real people from state records.

4
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:25,368
So how does the team run Aziz's journeys through MyID, without the data of a real person, and without one test record ever reaching production?

5
00:00:25,418 --> 00:00:35,712
In Lesson 7, simulators stood in for each outside service, and in Lesson 37, the panel kept every change to configuration under maker-checker.

6
00:00:35,762 --> 00:00:41,160
This lesson puts those pieces into two zones, and gives Aziz a test identity.

7
00:00:41,210 --> 00:00:43,032
First, the zones.

8
00:00:43,082 --> 00:00:47,136
The bank runs heeyID as two separate installations.

9
00:00:47,186 --> 00:00:52,512
Stage lives in the test zone of the bank, and production lives in the production zone.

10
00:00:52,562 --> 00:00:58,152
Each one has its own MyID credentials, its own connections to the bank's services, and its own data.

11
00:00:58,202 --> 00:01:05,184
Stage connects only to the MyID test environment, and production connects only to the MyID production environment.

12
00:01:05,234 --> 00:01:11,064
Neither installation can reach the environment of the other, and there is no network route between the zones.

13
00:01:11,114 --> 00:01:14,784
Only configuration moves from stage to production.

14
00:01:14,834 --> 00:01:19,008
Customers, devices, sessions and audit events never move.

15
00:01:19,058 --> 00:01:21,000
Second, the test identity.

16
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:25,680
MyID has a test environment, with test identities registered there.

17
00:01:25,730 --> 00:01:27,816
The stage configuration lists them.

18
00:01:27,866 --> 00:01:37,152
Each one has a personal identification number, or a document series and number, and the result that it must give: success, or a named failure reason.

19
00:01:37,202 --> 00:01:45,528
For example, one identity succeeds, another fails because the face does not match, and another fails because the document has expired.

20
00:01:45,578 --> 00:01:50,184
So the acceptance tests run the same journeys with the same results, every time.

21
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:51,816
Third, the test flag.

22
00:01:51,866 --> 00:01:58,560
Each customer that the platform creates in stage uses a MyID test identity and carries a test flag.

23
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:01,848
Limits and reports never count test customers.

24
00:02:01,898 --> 00:02:07,200
In production, every customer is a real person that the MyID production service proved.

25
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:14,952
That includes the people the bank uses for its own checks: in production they are normal customers, under every rule of this course.

26
00:02:15,002 --> 00:02:19,512
I think that rule is the honest one: production has no back door called test.

27
00:02:19,562 --> 00:02:22,200
Fourth, the guards that keep the zones apart.

28
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:28,536
A production build does not start when its MyID host is a test host, or when test customers are allowed.

29
00:02:28,586 --> 00:02:34,080
In the production zone, the database itself refuses a customer row with the test flag.

30
00:02:34,130 --> 00:02:42,360
And the move from stage to production is only a difference in Helm values, which a second person approves, so no database content moves.

31
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:44,280
Fifth, the simulators.

32
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:53,592
Every acceptance test can also run with no MyID, no SMS service, no push service, no customer lookup and no antifraud system.

33
00:02:53,642 --> 00:02:58,824
The simulators give each reason code and each answer that the real services can give.

34
00:02:58,874 --> 00:03:03,312
So a developer can run the whole suite on a laptop, with no internet.

35
00:03:03,362 --> 00:03:09,672
Each night, a second run goes against the MyID test environment and adds to the simulated run.

36
00:03:09,722 --> 00:03:11,544
Back to Aziz.

37
00:03:11,594 --> 00:03:15,096
In stage, he is a test identity with a known result.

38
00:03:15,146 --> 00:03:19,128
On a developer's laptop, he is a simulator that never gets tired.

39
00:03:19,178 --> 00:03:23,424
And in production, he does not exist at all, which is exactly right.

40
00:03:23,474 --> 00:03:29,376
To recap: two zones, two installations, two MyID environments, and no route between them.

41
00:03:29,426 --> 00:03:37,416
Test identities with expected results, a test flag that limits and reports ignore, and guards that stop a test value in production.

42
00:03:37,466 --> 00:03:40,368
But one question is still open for the whole course.

43
00:03:40,418 --> 00:03:46,536
Who builds these two installations in a new bank, from nothing, and how do we know that the build is done?

44
00:03:46,586 --> 00:03:50,016
Next, the last lesson: installing heeyID.
